205:// ---- Storage arithmetic ---------------------------------------------------
206:// A real migration filled a 25 GB root disk to 92% and two of three compose
207:// projects died with `no space left on device`, because the pre-flight only
208:// counted the ~11 GB file copy. Docker needs FAR more than the image bytes:
209:// `docker load`/`docker pull` writes the compressed stream, the content store
210:// (/var/lib/containerd/io.containerd.content.v1.content) keeps the layer blobs
211:// AND the snapshotter unpacks them again, and any `build` adds a build cache on
212:// top (measured: `docker builder prune -af` reclaimed 7.1 GB right after two
213:// image builds). So the allowance is 2× the source's image bytes plus a fixed
214:// floor, and free space is re-checked immediately before the image phase and
215:// again before every single image — never just once at the start.
216:const IMAGE_OVERHEAD_FACTOR = 2;
217:const FS_HEADROOM = 2 * GiB; // OS + apt + logs + dump files
218:const IMAGE_PHASE_FLOOR = 1.5 * GiB; // refuse to start an image op below this
219:const MAX_IMAGE_TRANSFER_BYTES = 24 * GiB; // total docker save/load budget
220:const MAX_SINGLE_IMAGE_BYTES = 12 * GiB;
221:// Where an attached-but-unused block device gets mounted, and the two Docker
222:// stores that are relocated onto it. `/var/lib/containerd` is a SEPARATE store
223:// from `/var/lib/docker` — with the containerd image store that get.docker.com
224:// installs on Ubuntu 22.04, layers and build ingest live under containerd, so
225:// relocating only /var/lib/docker leaves the root disk filling up anyway (it hit
226:// 100% in the field with 9.4 GB sitting in /var/lib/containerd).
227:const EXTRA_DISK_MOUNT = "/mnt/nxeon-data";
228:const DOCKER_STORES = ["/var/lib/docker", "/var/lib/containerd"] as const;
229:const MIN_EXTRA_DISK_BYTES = 4 * GiB;
230:/**
231: * Block-storage volumes are PORTABLE: the Volumes UI promises they can be
232: * attached to any of the customer's running servers, and that is what every
233: * major provider does (EBS, DigitalOcean, Hetzner, Linode, Vultr). Relocating
234: * `/var/lib/docker` + `/var/lib/containerd` onto a volume silently converts a
235: * detachable add-on into one the server cannot boot without — so the engine
236: * NEVER does it on its own. It happens only with explicit consent
237: * (`useVolumeForDocker`), and when it does, this is said plainly in the run log
238: * AND in the final summary.
239: *
240: * Deliberately NOT implemented: LVM pooling of several volumes into one
241: * filesystem. It would give a customer the combined capacity, but it welds the
242: * disks together permanently and destroys portability for ALL of them at once.
243: */
244:const VOLUME_IN_USE_WARNING =
245:  "IMPORTANT: this volume is now in use by the server — " +
246:  `${DOCKER_STORES.join(" + ")} live on it. ` +
247:  "It cannot be detached or moved to another server while the server depends on it: " +
248:  "detaching it will break Docker. Move the data back onto the system disk first if you ever want it back.";
249:// A target this small cannot build container images without starving itself — a
250:// 1 vCPU / 2 GB box wedged so hard during a .NET + Astro build that the QEMU
251:// guest agent stopped answering. We do not refuse, we warn loudly and keep our
252:// own SSH channel authoritative.
253:const SMALL_TARGET_VCPU = 2;
254:const SMALL_TARGET_RAM_BYTES = 4 * GiB;
